  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nSatellite imagery this afternoon showcases the cold front that \nbrought our overnight showers moving through WI with a field of \ncumulus across central and northern Minnesota, with an area between \nthe front and cumulus showcasing some wispy smoke from wildfires \nover the western Rockies. Little of this smoke is expected to reach \nthe surface but it could produce enough of a sky mask to knock high \ntemperatures down by a degree or two this afternoon, with \ncomfortable low to mid 70s expected. Low temperatures tonight will \nlikewise be comfortable in the low 50s outside of the urban heat \nisland, with upper 50s within the Twin Cities. The upper level smoke \nis not expected to make it to the surface, so you should be safe to \nopen the windows and get some fresh air into your home should you \nchoose to do so. Tomorrow looks to be a bit warmer than today with \nnear 80 high temperatures and mostly sunny skies, with the breeziest \nwinds this week arriving on Thursday with some frequent gusts around \n20mph out of the south ahead of our next cold front dropping down \nlater in the day on Friday.\n\nQuiet weather is expected to continue today and through Thursday \nuntil our next precipitation chances arrive Friday evening/night. \nEnsembles continue to show very different ideas on upcoming \ntemperatures with the GEFS wanting a push back into the low 90s by \nFriday and into the weekend with the much cooler EPS peaking in the \nmid 80s. This also shows within the longer range forecast with some \nsporadic storm chances arriving this weekend with fairly low \nconfidence given the competing solutions. The warmer GEFS would \nfavor more instability and with any sort of forcing would promote \nsome stronger storms, however could also result in more capping if \nthe lower-mid levels heat up more. Speaking of forcing, a broad \nfrontal boundary will slow move from north to south across Minnesota \nbeginning Friday night and lasting into early next week, with \nrelatively weak forcing given the lack of a strong surface low and \nweak upper level reinforcement. We are certainly showcasing the \nsouthwestern retreat of the heat dome in this situation, with our \nupper level flow turning more northwesterly causing our upstream \nflow to come from the PAC NW instead of to the south, limiting our \nmoisture and also introducing a likelihood of some upper level smoke \nhelping to knock down temperatures. Our expectation would be to see \ntemperatures settle closer to the low 80s as we head into mid \nAugust, which would be around our average high temperature for this \ntime of year if slightly above normal.",
